A||N||19||bwayne||Power Steering Kit Available anymore?||||5||
Z||000000||bwayne||11-06-1999||11:49 PM||||I'm new to Fiero's so you experts are probably bored by the question, but are there any power steering kits available for my '86 coupe, L4?  The car is for my daughter and she can't seem to get the knack for manual steering.||||reg||5
Z||000001||FieroGT||11-07-1999||12:07 AM||||Last time I heard, there was a conversion company that made power steering kits for Fieros. This was about 4 years ago, or so. There were very few kits produced. Cost was over $1000, and it was made using modified Chevette parts. Exactly how the parts were modified, I don't know. I guess the cheaper solution for you would be to get skinnier front tires, or a gym membership for the daughter     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> .<P>There are also countless rumors about a fabled few '88's leaving the factory equipped with power steering, or Fieros being assembled and later stripped of the power steering components before they left the factory. So far, rumors are all they have proven to be. There were plans to equip '88 Fieros with optional power steering, but upon cancellation of the car in August of '88, it was considered to be too expensive to keep the necessary parts in stock for years to come for comparatively few vehicles produced. I believe that the 22P Parts Manual has illustrations of the power steering rack assembly with the part numbers blacked out. Some interesting tidbits, and probably more information that you wanted to know.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 11-07-1999).]<P>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 11-07-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by FieroGT (edited 11-07-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Shiner||11-07-1999||01:06 AM||||Ever since I have been driving fieros, I can't stand driving cars with power steering!  They feel so dead, while the fiero's manual steering is like having a tie rod in each hand!  It's really one of my favorite things about the fiero!||||reg||7
Z||000003||Standard||11-07-1999||04:19 AM||||I just read something a little while ago that says that the 88's have mounts for power steering, just no equipment installed.  forgot where i saw it...||||reg||4
Z||000004||Old Lar||11-07-1999||05:52 PM||||I saw a Fiero with a Mercury Mekur power steering unit installed.  The guy (?) said it worked.  This was at the Rutland, Mass FOCONE fall show.  Maybe Tim Perrego knows who had the 87 GT with the power steering.||||reg||1
Z||000005||ChadMan||11-07-1999||06:29 PM||||I think there must have been at least one Fiero (a yellow 88 GT or Formula) built with power steering, because it's shown on my Motorweek Fiero tape.  At the 88 press introduction event at Mid-Ohio, Motorweek taped a woman sitting in a yellow Fiero turning the wheel back and forth in a demonstration, and she doesn't appear to be working that hard.  Hmmm...the Loch Ness Monster, Bigfoot, and now a yellow 88 with power steering.  It's probably in some GM warehouse somewhere under a tarp!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Standard||11-07-1999||07:21 PM||||Reminds me of the ending of Indiana Jones and the Lost Ark... goverment takes it, puts it in a box, stamps a number on it, and stores it in a huge warehouse...||||reg||10
Z||000007||Cooter||11-07-1999||08:52 PM||||I have been searching my car mag. collection for an article about GM's lost test cars. One mag. had where a "lost" 82 Trans Am with the 1LE package (showroom stock race car)  was found in a warehouse with near zero miles on it. Another has pic's and articles about the 4 wheel steer Z-28, the dual engine (twin 3.1's) citation etc. . . Pretty cool stuff that just got put away to never be used.||||reg||14
Z||000008||Tim||11-07-1999||11:00 PM||||Info from 1989 Mitchell "crash book" page 34, no pictures.<P>Gear Assembly,Power       26003987   $350.00<BR>except Special Performance26003991   $350.00<BR>Pump, Power Steering      26003096   $642.00<BR>Reservoir, Steering       26000889   $ 67.25<BR>Cap, Reservoir            26008138   $ 12.20<BR>Hose Kit, Reservoir       26007593   $ 17.50<BR>Controller, (Electro/Hydraulic)<BR>                          22068247   $260.00<BR>Bracket, Controller Mount 26000628   $ 50.00<BR>Rod Kit, Outer            26007039   $ 56.75<P>I understand the numbers are also in the 1988 GM parts books.||||reg||1
Z||000009||fiero56||11-25-1999||06:34 PM||||Just to resurrect a dead thread.  For those who know Rodney Dickman, THE acessory guy, he just got a FACTORY '88 power steering rack and all! He says he'll be showing it at the normal Fiero events and that it is NOT for sale.  Also says it is not possible for him to reproduce these things. Too bad, I know people who would love to have one. I also like not having power steering, but it would be a cool add on.||||reg||1
Z||000010||four50four||11-25-1999||06:41 PM||||did he get a whole fiero, or just the PS assembly?||||reg||1
Z||000011||fiero56||11-25-1999||09:41 PM||||To answer your question he has the rack.<P>I'm on the fiero.org list and he posted it there.  He says he was looking to suuply the Fiero community with steering rack rebuild parts.  He finally got the company who rebuilds these racks to order bushungs and such. After talking for a while, the guy said they also had a 1988 Fiero power steering rack that they recieved from GM back in '87.  Rodney promptly bought it and now has it at his house.  <BR>Apparently automakers sends this company new racks to be disasembled for R&D so they know what parts they can make and what has to be ordered from the outside. This rack had just been sitting on a shelf all this time.<BR>Rodney states it had been pressure tested and if installed today will still work.<BR>He says it is almost identacle to the regular racks.  Another difference he states is the steering ratio. Stock '88 is 3 1/3 turns lock to lock.  The power steering is 2 1/3 turns lock to lock.  That would be sweet to have.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-25-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Monkeyman||11-26-1999||03:49 AM||||There were 13 '88 Fieros produced with power steering.  I think 4 Formulas and 9 GTs.  It was an electric motor of some sort.  I have a friends father who just retired from GM.  He was a bean counter, but has a passion for Fieros.  Just received an e-mail from him last week about this as I was interested in putting power steering on my car.  He said that he drove one once and the handling at speeds faster than about 40mph was terrible.  VERY loose.  Like we don't have problems with the steering already.  The only way I'd ever have power steering on my Fiero is if someone could come up with a variable ratio unit.||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||11-26-1999||09:33 AM||||I never heard about the handling problems.  I could believe it though. Rumors I heard were that the system was too noisy, and they hadn't gotten a chance to quiet it down yet. <BR>I remember in my Autoweek video they talk about the p.s.  It doensn't have variable ratio, but did have variable assist.  Full assist at low speeds(parking lot etc) and almost no assist at highway speeds. I don't remember if they got to drive with the power steering or not, I'll have to pull out the video and see what they had to say about the "driving feel".||||reg||1
Z||000014||WKDFIRO||11-26-1999||10:40 AM||||Perhaps this person got a Fiero that was a practice test from the factory but power steering was never sold to the public. The only power steering I have ever seen was on a Zimmerman and it was running off of the water pump pulley. But for you 88 buffs out there, the wiring was set up for a steering unit! The electro hydraulic power steering unit by Saginaw was slated as a $275 option for mid year (April 88). It was tested by some magazines but never made production.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fierosound||11-26-1999||12:55 PM||||I've noticed many wishing they had power steering. I accidentally found the next best thing. MICHELIN ENERGY MXV4 tires. <BR>These are a "low energy" tire, meaning it has low internal rolling resistance built in. Therefore you use less fuel. As a side benefit, steering wheel effort is greatly reduced. (I stopped and checked to make sure they hadn't accidentally inflated the tires to 80PSI or something!!) I'm basically a one-handed driver, and the only times two hands are necessary are when parking. Even then, less "grunt" is required.<P>The tires are a performance tire 205R60-15 on front, 210R60-15 on rear. As I live in Canada I got all-season versions. Great handling, good ride, less road noise.<P>Highly recommeded. Anyone else riding on these. <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Mona||11-27-1999||08:25 PM||||Hey Fierosound,<BR>How Much did the Michelins run you?, if you don't mind me asking!<BR>I'm in need of new tires, but can't make up my mind on which ones<BR>Thanks!||||reg||5
Z||000017||Monkeyman||11-27-1999||11:53 PM||||Mona--Where in Indiana do you live?  I live in Wabash, there's at least one that lives in Ft. Wayne, 2 or 3 that live in Indy, and 1 that lives in Plymouth.  I'm sure there are alot more around here.<p>[This message has been edited by Monkeyman (edited 11-27-1999).]||||reg||5
Z||000018||Mona||11-28-1999||08:17 AM||||Hello Monkeyman!<BR>I Live 30 miles south of Terre Haute in Sullivan Co.<BR>There isn't very many Fiero's around here.<BR>A friend lives down the street has a 86 GT, He's the only person I know around here that has a Fiero.<BR>-Mona||||reg||10
Z||000019||bwayne||11-30-1999||08:29 PM||||Mona:  I looked up the Michelins at TireRack up in South Bend.  $107 each.  I was just down near you in Merom 2 weeks ago--If I'd known, I'd have stopped by to see your ride!<BR>(I also live in Plymouth, by the way, so there must be at least 2 in Plymouth!)||||reg||1
